{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "pitchzazz: live demo (10-min core path)",
  "description": "Seven stops, trimmed from the full 12-stop `.tours/pitchzazz-walkthrough.tour` for an actual 10-minute live demo with room for questions in between — roughly 60-90 seconds per stop, not a strict script. Picks the strongest, most self-contained evidence pieces (real-time safety, a real debugging story, a real perf investigation, one algorithm mechanism shown in full depth, the three-engine comparison, the hot-swap architecture, and the multi-validator story) and cuts supplementary depth (the Rust-prototype detour, the dedicated pitch-detection stop, and TD-PSOLA/Varispeed's individual mechanism stops — all three still get name-checked in stop 5's comparison). Everything cut here is still in the full tour and in `docs/ALGORITHMS.md`, including its acronym glossary, for any question this doesn't cover live.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "1. Never block, allocate, or lock on the audio thread",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/PluginProcessor.cpp",
      "line": 171,
      "pattern": "Everything below is real-time-safe by construction",
      "description": "This is `processBlock`, the actual audio callback in the C++/JUCE plugin: downmix into a fixed-size stack buffer (no heap allocation), then push into a lock-free FIFO (First In, First Out queue) — `juce::AbstractFifo`, atomic indices, no mutex, audited before use the same way the original Rust prototype's `ringbuf` was. Push/pop copy fewer samples than requested rather than blocking when the FIFO is full or empty. No lock, no blocking send, no allocation — the three things that would turn this callback into a source of audible glitches, avoided by construction.\n\n![Producer/consumer pipeline: audio callbacks only push/pop a lock-free ring buffer; the worker thread does all the real work](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nFull story: this class's doc comment in `PluginProcessor.h`, and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"Real-time audio safety\" section, which this C++ side mirrors."
    },
    {
      "title": "2. A real, subtle bug: the scale-quantization fix",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/Scale.cpp",
      "line": 55,
      "pattern": "int nearestInScaleMidi",
      "description": "Pitch class = note mod 12. If it's already in the scale, pass through unchanged; if not, search outward by semitone distance 1-6 (below first, then above) and return the first pitch class that belongs.\n\n![A MIDI note's pitch class is checked against the scale; if it doesn't belong, search outward by semitone distance, checking below then above, until one does](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThis avoids a real bug that stalled the original Rust prototype for months: the original hand-built a note lookup table sized to an assumption (`intervals.len() == notes.len()`) that didn't hold for every scale, so note-snapping was silently wrong for some scales — no crash, no error, just musically wrong output. Pitch detection and shifting both worked; this one part was quietly broken. The fix: read the scale's own `notes()` directly instead of reconstructing a parallel table. Full story: `crates/pitch-core/src/scale.rs`'s module doc, `docs/FINDINGS.md` #4."
    },
    {
      "title": "3. Perf investigation: budget miss to measured fix",
      "file": "docs/PERFORMANCE_LOG.md",
      "line": 33,
      "pattern": "Measured `PitchCorrector::process()` time",
      "description": "First live run: 40-60% over the real-time budget. Per-stage timing (not a single top-line number) showed it was entirely the pitch-shift stage — a leftover oversampling factor of 32 that had never been independently measured. Dropping it to 8 fixed it with margin.\n\nFull story: the four chained entries starting here, ending in a measured (not estimated) end-to-end latency number, plus `docs/FINDINGS.md` #5."
    },
    {
      "title": "4. How the phase vocoder shifts pitch",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchShifter.cpp",
      "line": 69,
      "pattern": "void PitchShifter::shiftPitch",
      "description": "Per-bin phase difference from the previous frame gives each bin's true instantaneous frequency (unwrapped mod 2π via the `qpd` trick). That frequency and its magnitude are relocated to bin' = round(k·shift) — literally moving spectral content to shift pitch — while phase is accumulated coherently across frames (`phaseSum`) so consecutive frames stay phase-locked. An inverse FFT and windowed overlap-add (hop = frame size / oversampling) resynthesize the signal.\n\n![Each frame's phase difference gives true frequency, which is relocated to a shifted bin and accumulated across frames before an inverse transform and windowed overlap-add](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nFull story: this file's comments on the FFT-normalization convention and the C++/Rust frame-size divergence; `docs/COMPARISON.md`'s FFT size constraints section."
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Three algorithms, three hot-swappable engines",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchEngineRegistry.cpp",
      "line": 10,
      "pattern": "const std::vector<EngineDescriptor>& availableEngines()",
      "description": "All three engines are registered right here, hot-swappable at runtime — three genuinely distinct algorithms: the phase vocoder just shown (also the Rust FFI engine, ported line-for-line — see `docs/COMPARISON.md`), TD-PSOLA (Pitch-Synchronous Overlap-Add), which re-spaces time-domain grains instead of touching frequency bins at all, and Varispeed + WSOLA, which stretches the signal then resamples it — the same physical effect as varispeed-ing a tape reel, formant shift included.\n\n![Phase vocoder shifts pitch by moving FFT bins, with latency fixed to the analysis window; TD-PSOLA shifts pitch by re-spacing time-domain grains at pitch marks, with latency bounded but pitch-dependent](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThe measured payoff: TD-PSOLA's latency is ~19-46% lower than the phase vocoder depending on rate and tuning, because it scales with pitch period, not a fixed sample count — though re-tuning either engine has flipped that ordering before (`docs/ALGORITHMS.md` section 5). The cost: a real, disclosed reconstruction artifact on non-stationary audio on both PSOLA (`docs/FINDINGS.md` #19-20) and Varispeed (`docs/FINDINGS.md` #23-24) — the price of depending on a period or correlation estimate rather than just a shift ratio."
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Hot-swap architecture + the 20-30x illusion",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp",
      "line": 74,
      "pattern": "void CorrectorWorker::requestEngineSwap",
      "description": "A new pitch-correction engine (Rust, C++ phase vocoder, C++ TD-PSOLA, or C++ Varispeed) is handed off via one atomic pointer exchange, picked up between blocks, never mid-`process()`.\n\n![Hot-swap: a new engine is stored into one atomic pointer, picked up by the worker thread between blocks, then blended in via a crossfade over four blocks](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThe first swap discarded the outgoing engine's warmed-up internal state, causing an audible glitch no unit test or plugin validator caught — `docs/FINDINGS.md` #14. Separately: a first-pass Rust-vs-C++ benchmark looked 20-30x apart. It was a debug-vs-release build mismatch, not a language difference — full numbers in `docs/COMPARISON.md`."
    },
    {
      "title": "7. One validator isn't enough",
      "file": "docs/FINDINGS.md",
      "line": 28,
      "pattern": "juce::Thread` destructor asserts (UB) if destroyed while still running",
      "description": "This row: a real crash bug — UB is Undefined Behavior — that passed PluginVal at strictness 5 cleanly, then was caught by Apple's own `auval` — run specifically because relying on one validator felt insufficient. Direct evidence two independently-implemented tools catch genuinely different bug classes.\n\nFull ledger: every other row in this table, plus the tooling breakdown further down."
    }
  ]
}
